opengl32: 32-bit programs: pointers, sizes and buffer offsets

Follows 0043 (a 32-bit program's memory is a window above 4 GB, and ULongToPtr() in a Unix
library adds the window's base). The OpenGL call layer's 32-bit thunks used ULongToPtr() for
everything the size of a pointer, which was harmless while the base was zero:

- a context, pbuffer or sync object the 32-bit side allocated is passed with
  UlongToHandle(), which adds nothing: it is a pointer and now gets the base (Direct3D 9 and
  older, which go through WineD3D's OpenGL renderer, could not create a device);
- sizes and offsets (GLintptr, GLsizeiptr, and arrays of them) are numbers and no longer go
  through ULongToPtr();
- the array of pointers itself (shader sources, uniform names) is read through the base;
- an argument that is an address, or an offset into the bound buffer object (vertex array
  pointers, the indices of indexed draws, indirect draw arguments, pixel data read or written)
  gets the base only while no buffer is bound to the binding point it is read through.

unix_thunks.c is a generated file. Its generator (make_opengl) is changed only for the first
point; the others were made in the generated file, so running make_opengl again would undo
them.

Checked with tests/runtime/d3d9-clear.c, d3d9-draw.c and gl-buffer-offsets.c built as 32-bit
(the last fails without the buffer rule).

